> Thanks to Bruce Foster for pointing out the `cm setsetuid` and `cm
> getsetuid` DCE commands to me. I haven't had a chance to try them yet,
> but they seem to do what I was hoping I could do with setuid files.


Some more information on the `cm setsetuid` DCE command. The `cm
setsetuid` DCE command must be executed by the Unix root user on each
client machine that wants to allow SETUID files in DFS for that client
machine. Because it makes changes to the cache manager data, and not
the real DFS files, it must be executed after each reload of the
client machine. The `cm setsetuid` DCE command does not change the
settings of the SETUID flag for a given file, it only allows the
`chmod` command the permission to set thus flag within the cache
manager data for the given file. Thus, after executing the `cm
setsetuid` DCE command for a given file, the root user must still
execute a `chmod u+s` command to turn on the SETUID flag for a given
file. Because this has to be done after each reboot of each client
machine, it can be placed in the machine startup script.



