In article <329C4C8A.167EB0E7@panoramix.rain.fr>, Tom Fischer <tfischer@panoramix.rain.fr> writes:

>How can I increase the space available for use- I'd like to reserve
>the 'safety' space to just a couple hundred MB...

As Hans Schwengeler said, use tunefs with -m.  A couple of hundred
MB for 10 GB is 2%, which is probably a safe number.  I worked
backwards from the capacity of the disks for which 10% was chosen
as the default, taking a typical file system arrangement and for
most disks at the time 10% was about 256 KB to 1 MB per cylinder
group.  You may want to see how many groups your file system has
(dumpfs) and adjust the value you chose accordingly.

News is the sort of I/O load that could be adversely affected by
poor file allocation.  As a file system fills up it gets harder
and harder to find "good" space for files and they get allocated
poorly or far away from related files.

