references: <55tbqt$77f@post.gsfc.nasa.gov>
organization: CERN European Lab for Particle Physics
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In <55tbqt$77f@post.gsfc.nasa.gov> fish@daacdev1.stx.com ("John R. Vanderpool") writes:

>DEC OSF1 V3.0 seems to not be able to allocate a single 10MB
>static array.  am i missing something obvious?

Yes: the definition of 'static array' in C.

>statically it fails:
>
>int main()
>{
>char total[5004][2168];

total is an automatic array, not a static array.  C has the keyword
'static'.  Guess why?

>printf( "foobar\n" );

Using printf (or any other variadic function) without a prototype in
scope invokes undefined behaviour.

>}
>
>Memory fault(coredump)

Increase your stacksize limit and even the version with the automatic
array will work.  Using large automatic arrays is a bad idea in the
first place, however.

>it seems to work dynamically though:
>
>#include <stdio.h>
>
>int main()
>{
>char *total;
>
>total=(char *)malloc( 5004*2168 );

Without your silly cast, the compiler would have warned you that you're
doing the wrong thing here: malloc is not a function returning int
(and it doesn't take an int argument) so a proper declaration is a must.

Dan
