In article <569118$at9@status.gen.nz>, netwin@world.std.com (Chris Pugmire) writes:

>I have a 'c' program, that writes a lot of files to a lot of
>directories, using  'open', 'close' 'write'  calls.  
>
>On intel linux it runs 6 times faster than on digital unix v4.  

It would help to know what kind of system and I/O subsystem.  Digital
UNIX is supported on many older Alpha systems that are slower than
newer Intel based systems.

>Can anyone tell me why, it appears that digital unix is blocking the
>process while it writes the files but linux does not.

I don't have access to source to verify, but I think Digital UNIX
does a synchronous flush on close.  If the files are small the
open/close time will easily dominate the I/O time.  If Linux
doesn't think enough of your data to ensure it on the disk after
the file is closed, then it certainly be faster.

Another possibility is how data writes are scheduled and retired.
On UFS (a Berkeley Fast File System implementation), writes which
end on a file system block boundary are immediately scheduled for
writing.  The I/O is still asynchronous, once the data is copied
into the kernel buffer.  The affect is that data trickles out to
the disk in a steady stream (or flood).  The kernel variable
delay_wbuffers controls this.  If set, all writes will be delayed
until a sync or the cache needs to be flushed to make room for other
data.

Less likely, but possible, is that the disks on the Intel system
have a write-cache enabled at the disk, which is making the writes
faster.  Digital doesn't ship any of its disks with the write cache
enabled, since it places the data to be stored on them at risk.  But
if you want to run the risk you may be able to enable the feature
through scu(8).

Finally, the Digital UNIX does support the option of mounting
file system for synchronous writes.  It may worth checking to
see how the file system is mounted.

>ChrisP,  netwin@world.std.com                

