The following is an ATOM tool that will pin-point unaligned loads and
stores to the source line.

To use this tool, you'll need to have ATOM installed. This comes on
the "complimentary products" CDROM. If you don't have a copy, you can
get it from gatekeeper.dec.com -- send me email and I'll forward you
directions. This specific ATOM tool might require the more recent
version of ATOM that supports multiple object files.

Anyhoo, once you have atom installed, unshar and go to the "extras"
subdirectory and run make. Then run make in the UnalignedLoads
directory.

This will leave you with an Inst.o and Anal.o file in the
"UnalignedLoads" directory.

Now, suppose your application is named "foo".

Build an insturmented version of foo like this:

	atom foo Inst.o Anal.o -o foo-unaligned

and then run "foo-unaligned" like you normally do (ideally in the same
directory, since it needs some files generated by running "atom").

This will then report the source/line locations that are causing your
unaligned loads (all of them). As a bonus it shows you where all of
your load and store instructions are being done (by procedure and
basic block). I'm attaching a sample output. Note that this will
identify problems that occur in system libraries.

This output doesn't show what you specficially had asked for - a
procedure back trace every time you get an unaligned load or store.

For the price of a case of good beer, I'll add that code to the
instrumentation output. Heck, for more beer, I'll develop the tool and
run ATOM over your (statically link) binary and ship it back to you so
you can run it and locate the bugs. This saves you the hassle of
installing ATOM. And, if you really want it, I'll install ATOM, hack
the tool and do some phone help showing you how to run it.  Can you
tell that University raises stink?

