bobobobobo@aol.com wrote:
> 
> Interested in anyones experience placing multiple tape devices on a scsi
> bus, and the performance or lack of under nsr/legato - various versions of
> Digital UNIX.
> 
> We have been told, that subsequent tape devices (dlt) will perform at 50%
> or less throughpout,  even though the bus throughput is never being
> realized. (ie the first device may do 8 gb an hour, the second 4 gb and
> hour the third 2 gb and hour, yet the theoritical max of a 20 mbs bus
> would be 72 gb and hour)
> 
> Would apprectiate comments, suggestions or experiences.

An experience. :) We're using NSR to backup from around 50 machines
(approx 200 Gb/week) onto one server, using a DLT-2000 (TZ87) drive.
This drive is part of a TL820 robot, and I've written scripts to mount
tapes on request, so loading tapes is not a problem.
Nearly all machines have FDDI.

I've investigated potential expansion paths, including the use of a
second drive.

Adding a second drive did result in a higher peak transfer rate, but I
was surprised to see that the overall backup time did not improve. I
found that high speeds were dependant on a large number of sensitive
factors.
- The network (obviously).
- The load on the clients.
- The number and size of the save-sets. If you have only a small number
of large save-sets, then they will probably end up being scheduled
inefficiently across the drives, meaning that you can have one drive
operating at full load, and the other idle.
- The 'pool' arrangement. Obviously if you're backing up to multiple
pools, then all backups to a certain pool must complete before you can
load a tape from the other pool.

My most recent tests showed an interesting limit being reached - with
all other bottlenecks removed (using FDDI from multiple (6) clients,
through fast-wide-diff SCSI to two drives), the load on the server
machine becomes the limiting factor. The various objects of NSR pass the
data through the loopback port (you can see the very large number of
packets with netstat), and the machine (an Alpha 3000/600) spends a lot
of time in system mode.

So my recommendation for expanding the system has been to add a second
server.

Hope this helps...

Matt
