These are two separate issues. In reverse order-  'lprm' already has the
setuid bit turned on. (see below) 'lprm' is designed to not allow the 
behavior you are seeking as it is counter to the design of a secure
system.

On the otherhand, Patrick's problem is that "Kernighan & Pike" is very
old.
The standard now is for scripts to not implement the suid bit , even
when
it is set.  His best bet is to either redo his 'script' in C or to
obtain
and use the 'sudo' public domain program.

