Tim Mooney wrote:
> 
> In article <328CC55E.41C6@w3.org>, Jim Gettys  <jg@w3.org> wrote:
> 
> > And current netscape Alpha implementations have java as well...
> 
> Of course, under Digital Unix 4.x there is some chicanery necessary to get
> Netscape Navigator's Java support to work...
> 

The Netscape Navigator Gold V3.0 kit for Digital UNIX is available at:

   http://www.unix.digital.com/products/internet/download.html

This kit includes a workaround to a problem in Netscape Navigator to
allow the Java support to work on Digital UNIX 4.x. I have include the
README.Digital file from the kit.

README.Digital

Java Problem and Workaround for Digital UNIX V4.0
=================================================

This kit contains a workaround for an incompatibility 
between Netscape Navigator and certain implementations
of X11R6, including the Digital UNIX implementation.

The problem is caused by Navigator's assumption that the
X Toolkit library (libXt) is always built to use the 
C runtime library routine select() instead of the 
C runtime library routine poll() for event handling.
For X implementations where this assumption does not
hold true (like Digital UNIX), the result is that
Java applets and the Java console do not function
within the Navigator - instead, they show up as empty
windows or spaces on the web page, and they never get 
painted or receive mouse or keyboard events. 

The problem occurs on V4.0 of Digital UNIX, the first
version of Digital UNIX to include an X11R6 version of
libXt that uses the poll() routine.  The supplied
workaround takes the form of a V4.0 version of libXt
built to use select() instead of poll(), and a script
("netscape") that checks if the operating system 
version is V4.0.  If it is, the script sets the 
LD_LIBRARY_PATH environment variable to point to the 
directory that the script was invoked from and then
invokes the real Navigator binary ("real-netscape")
from that same directory.  Note that this assumes
that the netscape script, the modified libXt.so,
and the real-netscape binary will always reside in the
same directory.  If they do not, the netscape script
will need to be modified appropriately.



