At least under 4.0 the graphical partitioning tool was a little too anal
about making sure you didn't have overlapping partitions, to the point
that you couldn't even make new partitions if you had already created
the 'a' partition at offset 0 (new partitions always started out at
offset 0 size 0).

I was able to get around this by doing the disk from the last partition
to the first, i.e. disabling all paritions except 'h', placing 'h' at
the end of the disk; creating partition 'g' right under 'h', etc. until
finally I placed partition 'a'.

But disklabel is probably much faster for the experienced UNIX admin,
especially since even in the graphical tool I ended up typing in the
correct partition offsets and sizes after it picked ones that apparently
corresponded to the positions of the partition boundaries on the graph.

