In article <329578C1.38AA@dsea.unipi.it>, Alessandro Coppelli >  Scenario
>  ========
>
>      Box Digital DEC3000 with OSF1 cdrom ( scsi 4 ) and hard disk 1.5
>      GB ( scsi 3 ).

>  What I must to do 

"DEC3000" describes three general types of systems; the Model 300
family, the desktop 400/600/700 systems and the deskside 500/800/900
model systems.  The answer will vary slightly for each.

The 400 class family and 500 class family have two SCSI adapters,
one for internal devices and one for external.  The easiest way to
add a device is on the external bus, because it will probably the
last amount of new hardware, unless the disk is a bare disk with
no enclosure.

For mounting a disk inside the box, you'll want a mounting bracket
appropriate to the system.  In the diskside systems, the disks were
mounted on their side and a bracket was essential to keep it from
falling to the bottom of the box.  The disks in the desktop 400
class box, sat flat, but could move around without a bracket.

You may want to go back to the vendor to see what they can provide
in the way of mounting hardware or enclosures.  I don't think we
ever sold empty brackets.

More generally, remove any internal terminators on the drive, since
the bus will typically be terminated at the controller end and at
the end of the physical bus.  If the drive isn't in an enclosure,
the drive will typically have a set of pins that are jumpered to
set the SCSI ID.  The drive documentation should indicate how to
do this.

Once the drive is installed, use the system console to verify you
can see the drive.  If the console firmware can't see, you have
a problem and should continue until it is solved.  The console
command "Show device" will be sufficient to show devices on the
internal and external bus.  All the 3000 family also support
additional SCSI adapters and you have to use a different command
to see what devices are on those:

>>>t tc# cnfg

where '#' is the TURBOchannel number of the additional adapter.

Once you have the disk setup so that the console correctly
identifies it, boot the system.  All versions of Digital UNIX
will be quite happy to probe the SCSI bus to find whatever
devices are there, irrelevent of what is built into the
kernel.  Some versions needed hints for other reason; what
devices are system disks, what device should have I/O stats
collected, etc, but these are fixed by V3.2C.

If the device is cleanly found by the kernel without errors,
you can create the special device file for using MAKEDEV:

	# cd /dev
	# ./MAKEDEV rz#

where this '#' is the drive number found from (bus * 8) + target-id.
The internal bus, is always 0 and the built-in external bus is #1.
Since your target-id will probably be zero, this give either rz0 or
rz8.  I think V4.0 will create the special device files for you
when it discovers tape or disk devices.

With the special device file you can label the drive and start
to use it.  If the drive is well behaved and follows SCSI conventions
for putting the drive geometry in the right mode pages, the disklabel
command can get the geometry from the drive.  You shouldn't need a
disktab entry:

# disklabel -wr /dev/rrz0c quantum

With the disk labeled, newfs or other file system creation will
get the information they need from the label:

# newfs /dev/rrz0c

# mkfdmn domain /dev/rz0c


