In article <3295C084.41C6@samoa.unice.fr>, "Gilles B." <gbilanci@samoa.unice.fr> writes:

>  How can I do to fix the length of the core file at 0 byte .
>Because when netscape bugs , I discover a big file .
>(sorry for my english)

Some things to try:

1.  See if your shell supports limiting the coredumpsize to zero
    bytes.  The C-shell on Digital UNIX V4.0 supports this:

	% limit coredumpsize 0

    The manual page for your shell should offer the necessary
    clues.

2.  Create a symbolic link called core in the directory where
    these core dumps typically show up.  Make it a link to
    /dev/null.  It will still a few moment to write the core
    dump, but it won't use space, unless /dev/null breaks.

3.  Use mknod to create a special device file with the same
    major/minor number as /dev/null, but called "core".  Thus,
    you'll have your own data sink.

