> 
> Before converting several existing UFS partitions on an AlphaStation
> running Digital Unix 3.2C to a single LSM volume that will be used as a
> single UFS partition, I would like to know if I would need to re-newfs
> this LSM volume if I had to add a new disk to the volume on a later
> date?  Has anybody had trouble doing this? Does adding a disk to the
> LSM volume somehow update the UFS structures?
> 
> I am aware that LSM has the capability to add/remove disks from
> existing volumes, but is the local filesystem layer capable of handling
> these changes?
> 

I posted a similar question some time ago, and received several
replies explaining that this was not possible (this is under OSF/1 3.0
- it may (I hope) have changed under later OS versions).  That is
although it's possible to increase the size of the logical disk, any
UFS file system on it *cannot* be inceased on the fly (as can be done,
for example, on the RS/6000 under SMIT).  I had to back up the file
system, add extra space to the disk using LSM, newfs a new file system
and then restore from backup.

This is still easier than using disklabel to rearrange partitions, but
it would be v. nice to be able to increase the UFS size on the fly.  I
believe that there are other file systems available that allow this.
Anyone?

