We just bought EZ RPC,  here's what we found in comparison to hand coding.
.



Features:

o - EZ-RPC handles the generation of ALL client side and server side data 
marshaling  code.  Including dispatching, stub code and custom XDR 
routines.  This allows the developers to concentrate on developing their 
application and instead of writing communications code.  With a clearly 
defined API, the application programmer must simply define that API and 
its data to our compiler using our "c-like" IDL language.  With a simple 
invocation of EZ-RPC, your API is "remoted". 

o - NobleNet  provide a rich set of C data types that can be marshaled.  
Virtually any C data type or user defined C data type (typedef) can be 
marshaled as a normal parameter to the defined RPC function.  
 
o - NN build convenience routines that gives the user a clean interface 
to control initialization, cleanup and RPC runtime mechanisms. (changing 
timeouts, creating and distroying CLIENT FDs, initializing the Windows 
environment, etc.) 

o NobleNet give you debugging features that allow you to pinpoint the 
routine in which failures occur . We also generate all "communications" 
related entry points in a separate stubs.c file which allows you to build 
and link your application without the need to travel the network.  
Because you can separate the application from its deployment on the 
network - you can easily isolate issues due to application environment 
versus communications environment.  This will save a great deal of time 
debugging.

o - NN generate a custom makefile for your client/server applciation.  
When once generated, it will be retained until removed.

o - NN provide numerous methods for you to participate in the marshaling 
code.  We allow you to replace default code generation with your custom 
code.  We allow you to build-in calls to your routines at many places 
within the generated code.

o -NN provide 5 special RPC types that allow you to design asynchronous 
client-server applications more readily. (FORKING, BATCH, NOWAIT, 
CALLBACK, THREADED RPC, and soon the ASYNC RPC)

o - NN provide you with automatic mallocing and freeing of XDR Memory.  
During the XDR  phase, we malloc client side XDR memory for any return 
values.  You can in turn use this memory during the execution of your 
program and free it calling one of our custom free routines.  On the 
serverside, EZ-RPC also mallocs XDR memory on your behalf.  By default 
this memory is automatically deallocated upon returning to the client.  
On those occasions where you need presistent serverside memory, EZ-RPC 
gives you the opportunity to use a custom allocation and deallocation 
scheme that will keep any memory around until you invoke a single free 
call, releasing all previous memory malloced using this method.  (See 
ez_mallocAM and ez_freeAM).   On the rare occasions where we can not 
automatically free a complex user defined structure, we provide 
convenience routines that will free them for you.  Our next release will 
automatically generate free routines customized to your RPCs, which you 
can invoke from your client main to free this memory.

o - NN  generate strict ANSI compilant code when needed.

o - run on more than 26 flavors of Unix, Windows, Win95, NT, Alpha NT, 
OS/2, Mac/OS, VMS,  VXWorks and OpenVMS.  

o -  run on over 20 different Winsock TCP stack vendors.

In short NN give you the freedom and flexibility you deserve when 
developing and deploying your application.

Because we give you such flexibility in allowing you to develop your 
application, you spend time concentrating on design of the application - 
not the restrictions you face in trying to marshal your data. 
 
To summarize:

If you deploy your client - server application using  MIDL  you must 
overcome the following issues:
1.  Manage your own memory.  (XDR mallocs and frees on your behalf - you 
must understand these conditions and manage both serverside and 
clientside memory accordingly.)
2.  You must develop and code all of your "stub code" and convenience 
routines. (setup, initialization, etc.)
3.  MIDL lacks suppport for the rich enviornment of complex data types 
that EZ RPC handles.
4.  There are greater difficulties in parsing ansi-C header files using 
MIDL.  EZ RPC  can read and parse any system header file.  If you need to 
participate at a lower level, we allow you to customize routines or call 
in custom functions at many places in the code, MIDL doesn�t.
5.  No support.  You are on your own, using MIDL.  On the other hand, 
NobleNet has some of the industries best software engineers who are 
responsible for supporting your effort to  successfully create and deploy 
your client/server application.
6.  Platform coverage.  EZ RPC supports a standard interface on over 50 
different supported enviornments.
7.  EZ RPC has a greater ease of use, ease of debugging, and a shorter 
learning curve, cutting development time by upwards of 90%.  While MIDL 
increase you overall development cycle.






