From: aa0026@dce-1.wayne.edu (Thomas R. Stevenson)
Subject: Why doesn't DFS support SETUID files?
Date: 1996/11/26
Message-ID: <vd3exw8o8s.fsf@dce-1.wayne.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 200922459
organization: Wayne State University
newsgroups: comp.soft-sys.dce,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1



Although there are many reasons to go with a DCE environment, our main
reason was the distribution of files using DFS. For the most part,
this is working very well, with one exception, SETUID files.

We have many commercial and public domain programs that we would like
to be able to distribute via DFS that require the Unix SETUID bit to
be on in order for the program to work. With the extended file access
that DFS gives us, it seems a natural location to store these
privileged programs, except that DFS doesn't support the SETUID bit on
files! This forces us to store these very privileged programs in a
less secure Unix file system. Because we can't use DFS to distribute
these files, we also have the extra head ache of keeping these files
in sync on different machines (which is something we wouldn't have to
worry about if they were stored in DFS).

I understand the necessity to make DFS very secure, but it's also
important to know that when vendors create artificial road blocks like
this, all it does it force their customers to use far less secure
means to achieve their goals. This restriction doesn't prevent
customers from creating SETUID programs. All it does is prevent
customers from storing these programs in a safe AND distribute
location in DFS! 

While I'm dreaming about this, it would be nice if there were two
(independent) SETUID flags for DFS files. The first SETUID flag would
set a normal Unix SETUID flag (would give the program normal Unix
privileges, but no DCE/DFS privileges). This would fix 99% of all the
problems we are having with the commercial and public domain programs
we are running. I could also see a second (independent) SETUID flag
that would set a DCE UID flag (would give the program DCE/DFS
privileges, but no Unix privileges). If the customer wanted both
types, they would just turn on both flags for a given file. This
second flag would be going beyond the normal Unix privileges that the
SETUID flag now gives users, so I might be able to see the case for
not allowing it, but I see no reason at all for not allowing the
normal Unix SETUID flag from being part of a DFS file.

