   Yesterday I received a new AlphaStation 500/333 running (I assume) the latest
Digital UNIX (uname -a output gives OSF1 node V4.0 464 alpha) and comes with 
the Common Desktop Environment. 

   Since installing this machine, I noticed that one of my cron jobs
failed last night (a nawk script). Further investigation revealed that
changing the field seperator to '=' (with {FS="="} ), does not work. It
ignores the new field seperator specification and just uses the default
tab and space seperators.

e.g.
  
  echo "physical memory = 128.00 megabytes." | nawk '{FS="="} {print $2}'

will give the output :-

memory

instead of :-

128.00 megabytes.

which is the expected output (and which is produced on earlier versions of
Digital UNIX). This also fails if you use gawk instead of nawk. However,
it works if you use awk! I know that in REAL UNIX nawk is supposed to 
replace awk (and in some cases it is linked so that they are one and
the same), so why is it that Digital has two seperate programs still?
I have worked around the problem by copying the older (working) versions
of nawk and gawk to the new machine since we have many scripts running using
nawk.  

Just thought someone might want to know.


