mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 3.0Gold (X11; I; OSF1 V4.0 alpha)


I've got 4.0 running on a bunch of 2100s.  It appears that the routing
tables have lots of host routes in them.  The systems are NOT running
routed, gated, or any other routing daemon - they simply use a default
static route.  When I run "route -n flush", the host routes all go away
until I put the default route back on.  If I flush the routes on a
couple of servers at the same time, and then watch which host routes
reappear, the two servers will have some host routes in common, but then
they'll each also have a bunch that are unique to themselves.  And
there's more: the host routes all appear to be associated with existing
TCP connections - which means that they're coming and going as well.

This is occurring on all of our 4.0 systems.  However, three systems in
particular have a greater number of host routes on them.  Two of these
three systems are high traffic web servers, and one of them is an irc
server.  When all of the web servers are brought down, the host routes
do not appear.

tcpdump reveals a small amount of icmp redirect traffic all coming off
of a single router in australia and destined for just the irc server. 
I've tried flushing the routing tables, then starting up tcpdump
elsewhere on the subnet to watch the traffic, and then finally putting
the default route back on to a particular server.  The reappearance of
host routes on the flushed server still far outpaces the icmp redirect
packets coming into the subnet (and besides, it's all coming from one
host and headed to only one host).

Anybody seen anything like this?

Andrew Kuan



