In article <3293346B.41C6@coyote.nis.newscorp.com>,
	Andrew Kuan <andykuan@coyote.nis.newscorp.com> writes:
>I've got 4.0 running on a bunch of 2100s.  It appears that the routing
>tables have lots of host routes in them.  The systems are NOT running
>routed, gated, or any other routing daemon - they simply use a default
>static route.  When I run "route -n flush", the host routes all go away
>until I put the default route back on.  If I flush the routes on a
>couple of servers at the same time, and then watch which host routes
>reappear, the two servers will have some host routes in common, but then
>they'll each also have a bunch that are unique to themselves.  And
>there's more: the host routes all appear to be associated with existing
>TCP connections - which means that they're coming and going as well.

This is probably due to the Path MTU support added to Digital UNIX V4.0.
Path MTU is a process where IP determines the largest size packet that
can be sent to a host thus traffic to that host can use larger packets
resulting in better network efficiency.

Each host route contains the MTU of the path to a host.  To test
this hypothesis, use "netstat -r -v" and see if the MTU is non-zero.

Path MTU can be controlled via the sysconfig(8) command.  The inet
subsystem contains the following Path MTU controls: pmtu_enabled,
pmtu_rt_check_intvl, pmtu_decrease_intvl, pmtu_increase_intvl.

Setting pmtu_enabled to 0 will disable Path MTU from being performed.

sysconfig -r inet pmtu_enabled=0

will disable it on the running system.

Adding the following to /etc/sysconfigtab will cause it to take
effect on every boot.

inet:
	pmtu_enabled=0
<blank-line>

