FWIW, we benchmarked several computer systems around this time last year. 
We were running on a large Sequoia (multi-processor, fault-tolerant unix 
box) and the hardware maint costs were a killer.  We found process that 
ran 17 hours on the Sequoia ran 15 on an IBM RS600, 10 on the HP and 2 
(yes 2!) on the DEC Alpha.

Consequentially, we are about a quarter of the way through migrating our 
IS on the a DEC Alpha 8400. The users love the speed; nightly batch 
processing (about half reporting, the rest split between batch updates 
and incomming EDI) is done before midnight instead of 7:00-8:00 am.

IMHO, before you go down the Oracle or Informix road, take a look at a 
product called Universe, from Vmark (http://www.vmark.com).  This runs 
the Pick database model, which is a post-relational, multi-dimensional, 
variable-length data structure (all the stuff the Oracle and the rest 
have finally gotten around to talking about as "the new future"). It has 
SQL, an and even easier to use and more powerful retrivial langauge (no 
messy joins), and runs a highly enhanced version of BASIC.  You can get a 
copy of the Pick FAQ at http://www.jes.com, and visit Pick Systems and 
http://www.picksys.com. Good luck, and thanks for listening to my $0.02.

MJG



