In article <01bbd05d$a6174760$99d3bb8b@pc-blg.nsc.com>, benjieg@corp.nsc.com 
says...
>
>Is it possible to insert a system reboot in a crontab script?
>I want to do a reboot in the middle of a script and then continue
>right where it left off.
>
>Thanks.
>

Not that I know of. The reboot part is easy: the pick-up-where-I-left-off is 
not -- when you reboot the system has no idea of what is was doing with before 
the reboot. Now, you may be able to hack something together: for instance, 
your script leaves behind some semaphore (in the form of a particular file 
somewhere)....somewhere in your system startup (varies depending on whether 
you are 9.x or 10.x) you could have a process that checks to see if this file 
exists. If it does, it performs some specific actions and deletes the 
semaphore file for next time.

To do this you will go from:

*script A:
commands
REBOOT
more commands

to

*script A:
commands
create "continue" file
REBOOT

*startup:
if "continue" file exists do script B

*script B:
more commands
purge "continue" file

Depending on the exact nature of your task, this may or may not be trivial
-- 
