Subject: UPdate on OSF/1 C2 Problem
Date: 1996/11/13
Message-ID: <56dkda$32t@nuke.csu.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 196531977
sender: marc@alpha4.cs.csubak.edu ()
organization: Cal. State Univ. Bakersfield
keywords: C2, protected
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



	I would like to thank both Pat Wilson and Booker C. Bense
for their help and advice to my previous post.  With this help
I have found out what had happened (twice) on our system and it 
may be worth repeating. 

	If a DEC Unix (nee OSF/1) on an Alpha is running C2 security 
and crashes during a user login, there is an excellent chance that that 
user, say his username is "kolson," will have his PROTECTED password
file: /tcb/files/auth/k/kolson clobbered! This happens because the
authorized login opens the file for read/update (it needs to change the
last successful/unsuccesful login time, etc...) without making a
temporary backup copy (what do we tell students about working
with important files?). UNFORTUNATELY THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE THAT THE 
ENCRYPTED PASSWORD IS STORED (under C2) and as we all know, if you are 
updating a file and the system crashes, the contents are usually hash 
(and this is exactly what I found (twice)).

	I believe that it is imperative that the system manager
make a backup copy of this file with root ownership, group auth,
and privileges 640 (even though the times and last terminal will 
eventually be out of date) and the backup should be invisible, 
say /tcb/files/auth/k/.kolson_backup , otherwise 
"/tcb/bin/authck -p" will complain. This is especially true
since "root" is in the same predicament.

	My final comment is that I would not really have expected
such sloppiness (storing low priority infomation such as time 
changes in the SAME file which contains the critical encryptions) 
from a firm like DEC. I have in general found the VAX family under 
Ultrix over the last 5 years to be an excellent cost-effective and
flexible platform with good security (especially if Kerberos is
used). I have also found DEC hardware to be of excellent quality.
I like the processing power of the AlphaStations but I worry that
the software is not as solid as it should be.

					Marc Thomas
					marc@ultrix6.cs.csubak.edu





