>> script on an OS that is already optimized for batch jobs - and take five
>> minutes every few months to make any required changes. Hey, it's their
>> choice. <grin>
>
>Unix, optimized for batch jobs?!  I don't think so!  MVS, sure.  VMS,
>ok, maybe.  But Unix?   An OS "optimzied for batch jobs" would have a
>decent batch scheduler as a built-in, not that at, batch, and cron crap.

MVS has no built in job scheduler. There's a special subsystem (to be
honest: you have to select jes2 or jes3) needed to even 'submit' a job.
jes3 has the ability to control simple job-nets: what job has to be
finished to release (not to start, you have to 'submit' all jobs at
once) a given job.

But it is a hard job to manage jobs this way, so usually companies buy a
separate scheduler product, either from IBM or from other comanies.

No doubt, MVS is one of the best systems if you have a lot of batch
processing. But a good scheduler tool is NOT built in. And some
scheduler tools are available for unix, too.

