Most of the stuff you mentioned is available for FREE on NT.  The Unix 
freeware that can't be compiled directly to NT has largely been ported 
already.  As far as batch commands go, you're not married to the DOS
.BAT file environment.  Perl and REXX are both available for NT (Perl is
definitely free and I believe there's a free REXX.  Microsoft throws in
a REXX interpreter with the NT resource kit).

In addition, with NT, you have access to all the great DOS/Windows 
development tools, such as Delphi, Visual Basic, Visual C++, etc.  VC++
is overkill for quick'n'dirty batch jobs, but VB and Delphi are certainly
easy enough to use for that kind of thing, and they both have built-in
scheduling capabilities.

The more I use NT, the more I like it.  The tools I'm used to using in 
Unix are either in NT out of the box or are easily available for free.

--Paul



