Steve VanDevender <stevev@hexadecimal.uoregon.edu> writes:

>Linux is very good at caching files in memory, to the point that my
>486-66 running Linux gets surprisingly comparable numbers compared to my
>AlphaStation 255/233 running Digital UNIX.  Linux also has substantially
>lower system call overhead.

>  BYTE UNIX Benchmarks (Version 3.11)
>  System -- Linux tzadkiel 2.0.25 #55 Fri Nov 8 15:36:41 PST 1996 i486
>  [ 486DX2/66, Fujitsu 1 GB SCSI disk ]

>File Read  (10 seconds)                   78067.0 KBps  (10 secs, 6 samples)
>File Write (10 seconds)                   10500.0 KBps  (10 secs, 6 samples)
>File Copy  (10 seconds)                    1403.0 KBps  (10 secs, 6 samples)
>File Read  (30 seconds)                   81780.0 KBps  (30 secs, 6 samples)
>File Write (30 seconds)                    9461.0 KBps  (30 secs, 6 samples)
>File Copy  (30 seconds)                    1423.0 KBps  (30 secs, 6 samples)

File write speeds of > 9 MB/sec from a 486 box?  That sounds more like
writing to cache rather to disk.  Big vendors tend to get in big
trouble if they leave too much data in volatile caches, hence our
support of Presto boards and some raid controllers with battery
backed caches.

Chris P's system may not be waiting for all the metadata of file
creates and deletes to make it to disk.  You could check this
by writing a program that creates a lot of files, runing it, and
shutting off power.  Print the file names to the screen, then compare
where it was at power off to what you have after fsck is done.
