x-mailer: Mozilla 3.0 (X11; I; SunOS 4.1.3_U1 sun4c)


Paul Wylie wrote:
> 
> Holger Marzen <hm@bluebell.saar.de> wrote:
> : Compared with NT's *.bat-files (yes, the same crap like MS-DOS) every
> : other unix-like OS is a highly sophisticated batch processing OS. It
> : doesn't count that there are csh, ksh etc. for NT.

> Most of the stuff you mentioned is available for FREE on NT.  The Unix
> freeware that can't be compiled directly to NT has largely been ported
> already.  As far as batch commands go, you're not married to the DOS
> .BAT file environment.  Perl and REXX are both available for NT (Perl is
> definitely free and I believe there's a free REXX.  Microsoft throws in
> a REXX interpreter with the NT resource kit).

Missing the point, I think... The thing with *ix is that all the
tools are bundled with the OS. Even a c-compiler with lots of useful
tools for developing have (until recently) been somthing that comes
with ALL *ix-distributions. And it don't matter how easy it is to
get a product, or how free it is. It's bound to generate problems
anyway. Many free products take HOURS (that's money, too) to get
running and you do not have a reliable vendor if (or when) other
problems occur. And you might end up with different behaviour for the
same operating environment on different installations. There is
also a potential virus in every "free" product you install. Even
if it not is intended to be harmful, it can really trash a customized
installation (different from the authors installation, that is).

Another question is why you should run NT, and then use it as an
*ix environment. Don't make sense, I think. Many people forgets
that *ix and NT is NOT comparable on every point. For example NT
is a SINGLE USER environment, and *ix is not. It's a MULTI USER
environment. That is quite a differens, I think.

> In addition, with NT, you have access to all the great DOS/Windows
> development tools, such as Delphi, Visual Basic, Visual C++, etc.  VC++
> is overkill for quick'n'dirty batch jobs, but VB and Delphi are certainly
> easy enough to use for that kind of thing, and they both have built-in
> scheduling capabilities.

I agree that the Windows environment have many sophisticated tools
for developing. But you have such products for *ix too, if you like
to go beyond the operating system itself.

> The more I use NT, the more I like it.  The tools I'm used to using in
> Unix are either in NT out of the box or are easily available for free.

Good for you. I also use NT and I miss a lot of things, actually. But
then again, we propably are "used to using" different tools in *ix.

/magnus



