Frantisek Niscak <niscak@gauss.ee.ualberta.ca> writes:

> 	Our root file system is at 100%. The reason seems to
> be that /proc takes almost 1GB. It contains images of running
> processes and typical size is between 15 and 25 MB.

Is your root filesystem over 1G in size?  When you kill processes do you
have more free space in /?

/proc doesn't actually take up disk space because it's not a real
filesystem.  The files in /proc are not stored on disk but instead are
managed by the kernel, and their sizes correspond to the virtual (not
physical) address space used by each process.

> 	I have compared it with Solaris 2.5 and could see that
> /proc took only about 50Mb for all processes.

/proc has different semantics in Solaris and Digital UNIX.

> 	Can somebody tell me if the size of processes images
> in /proc can be controlled and how ?

Run smaller processes.

> 	Also, could I reboot without /proc being mounted ?

I think you'll find you wouldn't have any more free space in / if you
did.



