Hello all,

  The lab where I work purchased seven NEC MultiSpin 6Xe CD-ROM drives.  They
use a standard SCSI-2 interface.  The intent was to hook them up to an
AlphaServer 2100 4/275.  It runs OSF/1 version 3.2.  I've been unable to get
the drives to mount a CD-ROM.  The mount command just prints out 'I/O error'. 
The SRM console and the OSF/1 'scu' utility find the drives, and can
"exercise" the drives without trouble.  The scu 'eject' command also works
without a hitch.  Only trying to actually use the drives is what doesn't work. 

  I did some checking, and NEC reports the drive uses a 2048 byte block size
by default, and OSF/1 needs a 512 byte block size.  NEC says they do not
support OSF/1, and DEC says they don't support NEC (helpful bunch, aren't
they).  In theory, one can send a command of some sort to the drive to tell it
to switch to a 512 byte block size.  Any ideas on a way to do this? 

  I tried editing the /sys/data/cam_data.c file, creating an entry for these
drives (with a 2048 byte block size), and rebuilding the kernel.  That didn't
seem to help. However, I'm not entirely sure OSF/1 is using that entry.  Does
anyone know of a way to check if OSF/1 is using a user-defined cam_data.c
entry?  I worry that it isn't matching the SCSI ID string. 

  Any ideas would be greatly appreciated.

