In article <55lksu$ibc@ncar.ucar.edu>,
Steve Emmerson <steve@unidata.ucar.edu> wrote:
> Hi,
> 
> After switching to Digital UNIX V4.0A, my X server (/usr/bin/X11/X) now
> takes over 99% of the CPU when the system is otherwise idle.  I get this
> information from top(1), ps(1), and xload(1).
>
> Any ideas why?

Because the X server in 4.0 & 4.0a is based on X11R6 rather than X11R5, and
it appears to have some remaining bugs.  :-)

Seriously, the same thing often happens to me too, so you're not alone.
It's probably no comfort to you to know that the problem isn't as bad under
4.0a as it was under 4.0.

Under 4.0 I also frequently crashed my X server.  Since upgrading to 4.0a the
crashes aren't as frequent, but they do still happen.

I haven't yet reported the problem to DEC, but I'm planning on doing so.
I'm working on finding a way to reliably reproduce both the "CPU-sucking" 
problem and the X server crash problem.  So far, using either "display" 
or "animate" from the ImageMagick package (I have version 3.7.6) seems to 
be my best bet for crashing the X server.  "xlockmore" seems to be the
application that most often causes the "CPU-sucking" problem, though I run
xlockmore in random mode so I don't yet know which screen saver mode it is
that causes the problem.

I've gotten into the habit of logging out when I notice the problem and then
restarting X at dtlogin.  It's a pain, but it clears up the problem for a
little while anyway.

> System Details:
>     $ uname -a
>     OSF1 ernie.unidata.ucar.edu V4.0 464 alpha

Are you, by any chance, getting some/all of your fonts from a font server?
Several of the crashes I had under 4.0 appear to have been in X calls having
to do with the font server.  I have yet to get a good core file for Xdec under
4.0a, so I don't know if the same is still true.

Tim
